17592186045417] [17592186096745 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096745 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096746 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1045 "Reduced Row Echelon Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096857 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b8439109-03d0-56d9-8a44-f5a5b744e4b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+z=2 \\\\  & x+2y+z=0 \\\\  & x+y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb5d2e37-01c6-52dc-86a3-0e3d22b79684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097174 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=3 \\\\  & y=-2 \\\\  & z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3$, $y=-2$, and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨3,-2,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097522 #edb/ref 1061 17209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78885948-fa6f-5f46-8f96-e5da861960dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097523 #edb/ref 1061 17210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "278dc07d-f5da-5098-a755-d5a72662e2ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097524 #edb/ref 1061 17211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f602ae0-3af8-588f-bdb5-c0995e19908f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097525 #edb/ref 1061 17212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c80be1fb-c96f-5733-8d79-5c777f840ef9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097634 #edb/ref 1061 23346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3553e51-447b-5d4a-b387-1bc43d2a825d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097635 #edb/ref 1061 23347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4208bcd7-ee35-5977-8afd-7beb404e8b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097636 #edb/ref 1061 23416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4f6891c-3e25-562a-900b-9617b1000f05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097637 #edb/ref 1061 23417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "541dad41-3dc8-5018-acca-4d79ebbbda41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7c/7c2283c7de572acfc58b443499598554ecf3d26b1a13c48c80b8d6aadf7b576a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form, but we can transform it even further into the **reduced row echelon form** (RREF) using the standard **Gauss-Jordan** method. \n\nThe algorithm is similar to the algorithm of converting a matrix to row echelon form, except that we need to make *all* the pivot entries equal to $1$ and *all* the entries below *and above* a pivot (in a pivot column) equal to $0.$\n\nIn our case, we convert $M$ to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final matrix is in reduced row echelon form:\n\n- all the pivot entries are equal to $1$ (here we have $\\color{red}1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- all the entries below and above a pivot are equal to ${\\color{blue}0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1065 3160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce056e74-9707-52d3-b172-483ca14e1f8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1064 "For a $3\\times 3$ system with $3$ pivots, the Gauss-Jordan method can be described as follows:\n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n![](images/5c/5c0f5ac3d95843b142c90e8ce83378ea2b2ad693baba6670e0cde46a449f5225.svg)\n\n- We use the third row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{33}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{23} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{23})R_3$, and\n\n- if $a_{13} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{13})R_3$.\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/9c/9c817628a3c646a928f12548a109b8136bb0a5977fab6e4d18e972690d81159b.svg)\n\n**Note:** Unlike the row echelon form, the *reduced* row echelon form of a matrix is unique." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1065 3161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f74e6075-9c2a-5495-ab21-470711559f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1067 "General Algorithm (RREF)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1064 "When a $3\\times3$ system of equations has two pivot columns instead of three, we can still reduce its augmented matrix to reduced row echelon form. The process is similar to what we did before. \n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n ![](images/99/992cfaabdb3490e99553fbbdfee0089b399306e3e65720fc6a70014bfa35321a.svg)\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/3d/3da5751a339ff2ee85d424b42a72f5270f1be076407a33abd1b79e0f082639d5.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1065 3949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "baed23fb-560e-56ad-aea3-46f3728ebfff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With Two Pivots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to write the augmented matrix for a $3\\times 3$ system of linear equations with only one pivot in reduced row echelon form. \n\nLet's see an example of how this works." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1065 3950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3748ee3-9d5d-5a6a-808f-ef8f99cbd1aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With One Pivot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-2,3,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "083caf03-1501-5cba-8190-446a66b010a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264325 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨2,-3,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264325 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9af8bfe2-063c-5dba-b504-5816b98f9766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264598 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨2,-3,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264598 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264598 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8b913e9-5eb2-5d55-9bc3-2f3b88be543b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264902 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨3,-2,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da1a8356-2e88-513c-b7fa-151c2d4478c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265152 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-2,3,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265152 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265152 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5e3c0bf-feab-56d8-8788-d7833ba1031b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265440 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "71bd9945-c16d-52b1-9144-9035edc43bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+2z=5 \\\\  & 2x-y+7z=7 \\\\  & x-y+3z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ff5175c-29cf-5e1c-8b8a-50a809738a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266102 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=2 \\\\  & y=-3 \\\\  & z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=2$, $y=-3$, and $z=0.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨2,-3,0⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339766 #edb/ref 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339766 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339766 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47f1079e-5752-5de3-a9e1-e19c06fada97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339942 #edb/ref 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339942 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #